# ─── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────── the only requirement
# Any Postgres will do. Locally that can be one on your own machine:
# DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/kodukeel"
# DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:5432/kodukeel"
#
# Hosted (Vercel + Supabase): create a project at supabase.com, then use the
# dashboard's Connect panel. Both URLs come from the pooler.supabase.com host.
# Never use the direct db.<project-ref>.supabase.co host the dashboard offers
# first — it is IPv6-only and Vercel cannot reach it (P1001 on every build).
# DATABASE_URL is the transaction pooler (6543); ?pgbouncer=true is required
# or Prisma's prepared statements break against pgbouncer.
# DIRECT_URL is the session pooler (5432) — a full Postgres session, which is
# what lets `prisma db push` / `prisma migrate` run schema changes.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.xxxx:PASSWORD@aws-1-region.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.xxxx:PASSWORD@aws-1-region.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# ─── Sign-in (Supabase Auth + Google) — OPTIONAL ───────────────────────────
# Leave both blank to run in local mode: one learner, no accounts, everything in
# the database above. Fill them in and the app becomes multi-user with Google
# sign-in and every route gated. See docs/03-architecture.md ADR-013.
# Same Supabase project as above. Project Settings → API for these two — the
# anon/publishable key is designed to be public, it only ever authenticates
# who is signed in; it is never used to read or write app data directly.
# Google OAuth and the mailed sign-in link are both configured in the Supabase
# dashboard rather than here, and one of them needs more than a toggle:
# Supabase's built-in email service sends a couple of messages an hour for the
# whole project, so on a copy strangers use, set up your own SMTP under
# Project Settings -> Auth -> SMTP Settings or the second person to ask for a
# link does not get one. See README.md "Adding sign-in (multi-user)".
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://xxxx.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""

# A mailed sign-in link, so a Google account is not the price of entry. On
# by default. Set this to "off" only for a copy whose mail does not go out.
# Supabase's own sender is a couple of messages an hour for the whole project,
# which is fine while you are the only one asking and is not once anybody
# else is: set up your own SMTP under Project Settings -> Auth -> SMTP
# Settings before you tell people, and add https://<your-app>/auth/callback
# to the dashboard's Redirect URLs (Google needs the same entry).
EMAIL_SIGN_IN=""

# The one address this copy lives at, e.g. https://kodukeel.ee. Genuinely
# public: it is the name on the front of the site. A Vercel deployment answers
# on <app>.vercel.app as well as its domain, and sign-in cannot survive the
# difference, because the sign-in starts on one origin and Supabase sends the
# learner back to its Site URL wherever the origin they asked for is not on
# its Redirect URLs. With this set, a request on any other host is redirected
# here permanently, so there is one origin for the whole flow. Previews and
# localhost are left alone. Set the same address as the Site URL in Supabase
# (Authentication -> URL Configuration) and add <it>/auth/callback to the
# Redirect URLs there.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=""

# ─── Anu, the AI tutor (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Get a key at https://openrouter.ai → Keys → Create Key
# Without it, every other part of the app still works; only the tutor is off.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=""

# A SECOND PROVIDER IS AN AVAILABILITY FIX, NOT AN UPGRADE, AND IT IS FREE.
#
# The chain tries providers in order, Groq first, and walks past one that is
# throttled, having a bad minute, or out of credit. What it cannot walk past is
# the end of the chain. Groq leads because at $0.29/$0.59 per MTok it is a
# fortieth of the dearest link here and is not rate-limited the way a free
# model is: preferring a free model over it buys a 429 to save a hundredth of
# a cent. The dear keys are still last, which is the half of "free first" that
# was doing the real work. OpenRouter contributes one link per free model, so a
# chain four long can still be one account with one balance: when that balance
# ran out on the live deployment, all four answered 402 in the same second and
# Anu went down until it was topped up.
#
# Either of the two below is enough to prevent that, and neither asks for a
# card. Gemini is tried before any paid key and after the OpenRouter models;
# Groq leads the whole chain, for the reason above.
#
#   Groq    https://console.groq.com → API Keys. Free tier, no card.
#   Gemini  https://aistudio.google.com/apikey → Create API key. Free tier, no card.
#
# WORTH KNOWING BEFORE YOU PICK ONE: a free tier is usually free because the
# provider reserves the right to look at what goes through it. Google says
# plainly that free-tier Gemini content may be used to improve their models;
# check Groq's current terms too. Everything a learner types to Anu goes to
# whichever provider answers, which is what /privacy already tells them, so
# choose the one whose terms you are willing to stand behind, and put the paid
# keys below in instead if neither is.
GROQ_API_KEY=""
GEMINI_API_KEY=""

# Optional, and only if you have credit. These sit at the end of the chain,
# behind Groq and behind whatever free tiers an install has.
#
#   Anthropic  https://console.anthropic.com → API keys
#   OpenAI     https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""
OPENAI_API_KEY=""

# Only for an Anthropic key scoped to the organization rather than to one
# workspace. Those are refused without this, with a 400 naming the header, and
# the app reads that as the provider having a bad minute and quietly tries the
# next one: on a chain with a free provider in front, the Anthropic leg is
# simply dead with nothing on screen to say so. The ID is in the console under
# Settings, Workspaces, and looks like "wrkspc_01...".
#
# LEAVE IT UNSET for a workspace-scoped key. Sending it there names a workspace
# the key may not belong to, which Anthropic refuses in the other direction.
# ANTHROPIC_WORKSPACE_ID=""

# Optional. Each defaults to a current model on that provider's free tier, and
# each accepts a comma-separated list tried in order. Leave them unset unless a
# name below has been retired, in which case the provider's own model list is
# the place to look.
# GROQ_MODEL="openai/gpt-oss-120b,qwen/qwen3.8-27b"
# GEMINI_MODEL="gemini-flash-latest,gemini-3.6-flash"
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL="claude-sonnet-5"
# OPENAI_MODEL="gpt-4o-mini"

# ─── Which provider answers which job ──────────────────────────────────────
# Two of the keys above are routed by purpose rather than by position in the
# chain, because they were configured for two different reasons and "whichever
# answers first" would be a rate limiter making a cost decision.
#
#   Anu (/api/tutor)        Anthropic, ANTHROPIC_MODEL, default claude-sonnet-5.
#   Scene lines (/api/scene) Groq, SCENE_MODEL, default qwen/qwen3.8-27b.
#
# There is no fallback between them. If the Anthropic key is missing, out of
# credit or erroring, Anu says so and everything else keeps working; if Groq is,
# scenes play off their recorded and banked lines, which is how a deployment
# with no keys at all plays all fourteen of them. Neither can spend the other's
# balance and neither outage takes the other's feature down.
#
# SCENE_MODEL is separate from GROQ_MODEL on purpose. GROQ_MODEL configures the
# general chain; this one is a measured choice (npm run eval:composers), and
# inheriting a general setting would move scene composition off the model that
# was actually ranked, silently.
#SCENE_MODEL="qwen/qwen3.8-27b"
# LEAVE THIS UNSET unless you have credit and a reason. Anu already asks a
# chain of free models in order (lib/tutor/provider.ts), and setting this
# replaces that chain with whatever you name, so pinning one model is opting
# out of the fallback. A free model is rate-limited hard upstream, which is
# what the chain is for. Takes a comma-separated list if you want your own.
#
# Measured on this project's Estonian grammar questions (npm run eval:anu),
# 2026-08-29:
#   google/gemma-4-31b-it:free              6/6   the default, and free
#   openai/gpt-4o                           6/6   roughly half a cent a question
#   minimax/minimax-m3:free                 5/6   second in the chain
#   nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free  5/6   third in the chain
#   anthropic/claude-sonnet-5               5/6
#   openai/gpt-4o-mini                      5/6   invented a nonsense Estonian example
#
# This note used to say that `*:free` models were too rate-limited to rely on,
# and that is why the default here was a paid model, and that is why a fresh
# key with no credit on it made Anu answer nothing. It was an artefact of the
# eval: it never read the HTTP status, so a 429 was an empty answer and scored
# as six wrong ones. Read properly, the free model at the top of this list
# answers every question the paid one does.
#OPENROUTER_MODEL=""

# Scan a page needs a model that can look at a photograph. Scanning uses
# whatever is configured above unless one of these is set, deliberately:
# turning the camera on must never move a deployment onto a dearer model on its
# own. These apply to scanning and to nothing else.
#   OPENROUTER_VISION_MODEL="openai/gpt-4o"
# and the same escape hatch for the others, as GROQ_VISION_MODEL,
# ANTHROPIC_VISION_MODEL and OPENAI_VISION_MODEL.
#
# THIS SAID "THE FREE CHAIN ABOVE IS TEXT-ONLY" AND THAT IS NOT TRUE.
# qwen/qwen3.8-27b reports input modalities of text *and* image on Groq's own
# /v1/models, so on the chain this app now runs, scanning already reaches a
# model that can see without any override at all.
#
# It was also checked rather than assumed, because the point of the feature is
# reading Estonian and a common OCR failure is dropping the diacritics. Six
# attested sentences out of the dictionary carrying õ, ä, ö, ü and š were
# rendered as page images and transcribed: diacritics preserved on 6 of 6, and
# 5 of 6 byte-exact, the miss being a dropped final "s" rather than a mangled
# vowel. That one would fail matchEstonianForm and be surfaced for a person to
# correct, which is what ADR-021 is for.
#
# SET ONE OF THESE ONLY TO A MODEL THE ACCOUNT ACTUALLY SERVES. A name the
# provider does not have is a 404 on every scan and a silent fall through to
# the next provider, which is slower and dearer than doing nothing. Both Llama-4
# vision models were tried here and neither exists on this Groq account.
#GROQ_VISION_MODEL=""
#OPENROUTER_VISION_MODEL=""

# A conversation is the hardest reading job in this app and the one place a
# better model is worth paying for. It asks a model to follow a beginner in a
# language most models are thin on, inside a closed word list of a few hundred
# lemmas, and write one line that is about what the person actually said; the
# gate withholds anything reaching outside that list, so a model that cannot do
# it produces a stage direction rather than a conversation. Since ADR-025
# amendment 1 it is asked on every beat that carries content rather than only
# where the bank had nothing, so the difference is most of the module.
#
# Measured on 2026-09-05 with `npm run eval:composers`, which forces one free
# model at a time and judges with the shipped gate: on the free tier
# `qwen/qwen3.8-27b` and `gemini-3.6-flash` wrote clean in-scope lines,
# `groq/compound-mini` wrote statements where the beat wanted a question, and
# two of the three free OpenRouter models answered 429 to every request for the
# rest of the day. Availability rather than quality is what a free tier costs
# here, and the lines written for the scene are what covers it.
#   ANTHROPIC_SCENE_MODEL="claude-sonnet-5"
# A provider named here is asked *first* rather than after the free chain, since
# naming one is choosing it. Nothing is set by default, for the reason scanning
# sets nothing: opening a conversation must never move a free deployment onto a
# paid model on its own. A turn is one short answer, so this is the cheapest
# paid path in the app to run well.
#ANTHROPIC_SCENE_MODEL=""

# ─── Ekilex dictionary (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Free key from https://ekilex.ee → register → profile → API.
# Without it, search is limited to the built-in seeded set (its current size
# is shown in Settings → Dictionary), and sentences, dictation and the fuller
# mock exam stay thin because the seeded set carries almost no attested
# sentences. Settings → Dictionary walks through getting one.
EKILEX_API_KEY=""

# ─── Who runs this deployment ──────────────────────────────── set these three
# Not optional if anybody but you is going to use it. Kodukeel is software you
# install rather than a service with one address, so the person or school
# running this copy is the one legally answerable for the data in it: the
# controller under the GDPR and the service provider under the Estonian
# Information Society Services Act. Both want a name, a place and a direct
# electronic contact shown to the reader, and /privacy and /terms render
# exactly what is set here.
#
# Leave them blank and both pages say, in as many words, that the operator has
# not been named. That is deliberate. A page that quietly says nothing looks
# finished; a page that says it is missing gets fixed.
#
# The registry code is Estonia's eight-digit business register number and is
# only for a company. A parent running this for one family is still a
# controller and simply has no code, which is why it is the one that is
# genuinely optional. The VAT number is the same kind of thing: give it if you
# have one, because the Information Society Services Act asks a registered
# provider for it and the cost page quotes prices net of tax.
#
# One exception, and it is narrow. The installations this project itself
# publishes name their operator in lib/legal/operator.ts, keyed on the
# canonical host in NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL, because four variables in somebody's
# dashboard is a step outside the repository and it did not happen: kodukeel.ee
# ran for months telling its readers that nobody had been named. That table
# answers only for the hosts it names. Serve this from your own domain and you
# get the unset state, which is the honest answer for you, and these four
# variables are how you leave it. They win over the table either way.
OPERATOR_NAME=""
OPERATOR_ADDRESS=""
OPERATOR_EMAIL=""
OPERATOR_REGISTRY_CODE=""
OPERATOR_VAT_ID=""

# ─── Who may sign in (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Leave both blank and sign-up is open to anyone with a Google account — the
# per-user AI quota below, not a guest list, is what keeps an open door from
# becoming an open bill. Set either one to turn this deployment into a private
# instance for a class or a family. Comma-separated; matched on the whole
# domain, so "kool.ee" does not admit "evilkool.ee".
ALLOWED_EMAILS=""
ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=""

# ─── Enterprise sign-in, SAML 2.0 (optional) ───────────────────────────────
# The email domains a company's own identity provider answers for, so that
# somebody typing a work address on the sign-in screen is sent to the login
# page they already use every morning instead of being mailed a link. Comma or
# whitespace separated, a leading "@" is ignored, and the domain is matched
# whole from the last "@" in the address, so "kool.ee" does not hand
# "evilkool.ee" to somebody else's provider.
#
# This variable is the app's whole half of it. The provider itself is set up
# in the Supabase dashboard, which needs the service role key and the customer
# to hand over their SAML metadata URL or XML; nothing in this repository holds
# a certificate, and no Supabase key beyond the two public ones above is ever
# needed by this app's own code. Add each domain here as well, or the sign-in
# screen has no way to know which addresses to route.
#
# Ask for "full_name" in the SAML attribute mapping. Without it a name falls
# back to the local part of the address, so a roster reads "m.aasa" where it
# should read the person's name.
#
# ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS is the natural pairing: this decides how a domain
# signs in, and that decides whether anybody else may. A pilot usually wants
# both set to the same domains, so the deployment is the company's and the
# company's people go straight to their own login.
#
#   SSO_DOMAINS="firma.ee, firma.com"
SSO_DOMAINS=""

# ─── Who reviews suggested fixes (set this if anyone else uses it) ─────────
# Every dead end in the app offers to send a suggested fix: a missing word, a
# gloss that is the wrong sense, a form that is wrong, an answer marked wrong
# that was right, a screen that failed. They land in a review queue at
# /admin/suggestions, and accepting a dictionary correction there writes it
# into the entry every learner reads.
#
# So this is the shortest list in this file, and it is exact addresses only,
# comma-separated. No domain rule: "this school may sign in" and "this person
# may change what everybody reads" are different questions.
#
# Leave it blank on a deployment with sign-in and nobody reviews anything: the
# queue tells whoever opens it that no reviewer has been named, rather than
# showing an empty list that looks like an empty queue. Reports are still
# collected and nothing is lost. In local mode, with no sign-in configured,
# there is one learner on one machine and they review their own.
ADMIN_EMAILS=""

# ─── AI spend limits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The app is free to use and these are what keep it that way. Every one has a
# default and there is no way to turn metering off. The tutor is the only
# metered path: review, the dictionary, every drill and the deck are never
# limited. Set AI_DAILY_USD_GLOBAL="0" to stop AI spending entirely.
#
# AI_DAILY_CALLS_PER_USER is the tutor's allowance. The other two paths scale
# off it in lib/usage/ledger.ts, because a grader note and a cache miss on
# speech do not cost what a tutoring conversation costs: the writing grader
# gets three times this, and speech thirty times, since it is free and cached.
AI_BURST_CALLS="8"
AI_BURST_WINDOW_SECONDS="60"
AI_DAILY_CALLS_PER_USER="10"
AI_DAILY_USD_PER_USER="0.50"
AI_DAILY_USD_GLOBAL="3"

# A slice of that day for each of the two routed purposes, because one number
# cannot protect two balances that are not the same size. Both this and
# AI_DAILY_USD_GLOBAL have to hold, and a slice larger than the global is
# clamped down to it rather than believed.
#
# What constrains these is a balance rather than a bill. Groq and Anthropic are
# separate accounts, and the numbers below are sized so that what can reach the
# Anthropic one stays inside what that balance can afford, even on a day Groq
# never answers at all. Per-call costs are measured against the real prompts and
# the real published rates (Groq's come off its own /v1/models).
#
#   AI_DAILY_USD_TUTOR   $0.0144 an answer on claude-sonnet-5, always Anthropic.
#                        $0.10 is 6 answers a day across the whole deployment.
#   AI_DAILY_USD_SCENE   $0.0014 a composed turn on qwen/qwen3.8-27b. $2.00 is
#                        about 1,470 turns. Groq only: a scene can never spend
#                        the Anthropic balance, because there is no fallback
#                        between the two purposes.
#   AI_DAILY_USD_GRADER  $0.0015 a note on Groq, $0.0038 if Anthropic answers
#                        instead. $0.04 is 26 notes, or 10 on a day Groq is out.
#   AI_DAILY_USD_SCAN    $0.0040 a page on Groq, $0.0100 on Anthropic. $0.02 is
#                        5 scans, or 2 on a day Groq is out. It gives up the
#                        most headroom, because the README calls it a rare
#                        action and the arithmetic agrees.
#
# SCAN and GRADER are sized off their *dear* reading, so a total Groq outage
# cannot overrun the balance. That makes them stingy on an ordinary day, which
# is the honest cost of a cap denominated in dollars: only a per-provider cap
# could say "cheap here, dear there", and that is not built yet.
#
# What Anthropic actually sees: $0.10 a day normally, $0.16 with Groq out cold.
# On a $5 balance that is 50 days and 31 days. Speech has no slice, because it
# is free and a dollar budget cannot ration something that costs none.
#
# Raise the tutor one when the Anthropic balance is bigger. Note that the Groq
# side, at these numbers, is about $62 a month if every slice is reached.
# Every purpose now has Anthropic behind it as a last resort, and this is what
# stops that being a way to spend the balance without limit. On a day Groq never
# answers, a scene turn costs $0.0034 at Anthropic's rate, a grader note $0.0038
# and a page $0.0100, so $0.05 is about 14 turns, or 13 notes, or 5 pages. Thin
# on purpose: a limp rather than a spare leg.
#
# Past it the last resort is simply not offered, and each purpose degrades the
# way it already does with no key at all: a scene plays off its recorded and
# banked lines, the grader keeps the verdict it decided against the dictionary
# before any model was asked, and the scanner says so.
#
# What it costs the balance: Anu is $0.10 a day and is not fallback traffic, so
# the worst an outage adds is this. $0.10/day normally (50 days on $5) and
# $0.15/day with Groq out cold (33 days). Uncapped, the same outage would put
# SCENE's whole $2.00 slice through Anthropic: $2.10 a day, and $5 gone in
# under three. Set it to "0" to switch the last resort off entirely.
AI_DAILY_USD_FALLBACK="0.05"

AI_DAILY_USD_TUTOR="0.10"
AI_DAILY_USD_SCENE="2.00"
AI_DAILY_USD_GRADER="0.04"
AI_DAILY_USD_SCAN="0.02"

# The tail of the day's shared budget held back for people who have not asked
# anything yet, so a busy morning cannot leave a newcomer with a dead tutor.
# Once spending reaches it, anyone who has already had this many answers waits
# until midnight UTC and anyone who has not can still ask.
AI_GLOBAL_RESERVE_FRACTION="0.25"
AI_RESERVE_CALLS_PER_USER="3"

# ─── Aggregate metrics (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this and GET /api/metrics returns cohort retention, weekly actives and
# dictionary size, computed from the append-only review log. Nothing extra is
# collected about anybody to produce it, and nothing that leaves the endpoint
# identifies a person: cohorts smaller than five report their size but not
# their rates. Leave it unset and the route 404s.
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $METRICS_TOKEN" https://your-app/api/metrics
METRICS_TOKEN=""

# ─── Anonymous research export (optional) ──────────────────────────────
# Set this and GET /api/research returns how often each grammatical case, each
# stem change and each word is answered correctly, added up across everybody on
# this installation. Worked out from the same review log, so nothing extra is
# collected. It is meant to be sent to people who teach Estonian or study how it
# is learned: where a lot of learners go wrong is not something one classroom
# can measure, and this is.
#
# Nothing in the file rests on fewer than ten people or fifty answers, no one
# person may be more than half of a figure, counts are rounded and head counts
# are bands. Anyone can leave their own answers out in Settings. Read
# docs/19-research-export.md before sending the file to anybody, and read
# lib/research/corpus.ts before changing any of those numbers.
#
# A separate token from METRICS_TOKEN on purpose: that one is your own
# monitoring, this one reaches the whole corpus. Leave it unset and the route
# 404s, which is also the state in which no export can be produced at all.
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $RESEARCH_TOKEN" \
#        "https://your-app/api/research?format=csv" -o learner-errors.csv
RESEARCH_TOKEN=""

# ─── Shared audio cache (recommended when hosted) ──────────────────────────
# Without this, synthesised speech is cached on local disk — which on a
# serverless host is per-instance and wiped on every cold start, so every cold
# start re-asks TartuNLP for words it has already spoken. With it, one clip is
# fetched once for everyone, forever.
# It has a second job, and it is the one that matters legally: erasing an
# account means erasing the sign-in record too, and that lives in Supabase Auth
# rather than in this app's tables. Without this key the delete button removes
# every row and then tells the learner plainly that their email address is
# still held by the sign-in provider and who to ask. With it, that is gone too.
# Supabase dashboard → Project Settings → API → service_role key. SERVER ONLY:
# this key bypasses row-level security. CI fails the build if it reaches the
# browser bundle.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""
SUPABASE_AUDIO_BUCKET="audio"

# ─── Trusting a proxy's forwarded-for header (self-hosted only) ────────────
# On Vercel this is handled for you: the platform sets x-vercel-forwarded-for
# and overwrites it on every request, so it can be believed and this stays
# unset. Anywhere else, X-Forwarded-For is just a header, and a caller sitting
# directly in front of Node can write whatever they like in it.
#
# So it is not read unless you say it can be. That is not caution for its own
# sake: the rate limiter buckets an anonymous caller by address, and a caller
# who picks their own address gets a fresh allowance on every request — an
# unlimited number of limits, which is not a limit. Untrusted, every
# unattributed request shares one bucket instead, which is the honest shape
# for a deployment that genuinely cannot tell two strangers apart.
#
# Set this to "1" only if a reverse proxy in front of this app strips or
# overwrites incoming X-Forwarded-For and X-Real-IP headers. Signed-in
# requests are charged to the learner and never touch any of this.
TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=""

# ─── Error reporting (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this and every handled error is POSTed to it as JSON: where it happened,
# the message, and the opaque user id — never an email address, and never a
# value that looks like a credential, which lib/observability/report.ts strips
# before anything is sent.
#
# It is still a third party receiving personal data, so setting it puts one
# more recipient on /privacy, automatically, named as an error-reporting
# endpoint. That is the whole reason it is documented here rather than left as
# a variable somebody discovers in the source: an operator turning this on is
# making a disclosure decision, and the page makes it for them.
ERROR_WEBHOOK_URL=""

# ─── The news feed behind the dictionary's suggestion row (optional) ───────
# The empty state of /dictionary offers a dozen words worth looking up, and one
# of the three sources it draws them from is the front page of the news: words
# that are in the air today, resolved against this dictionary and offered as
# its own headwords. Nothing a feed writes reaches a card, a form or a screen —
# a headline proposes and matchEstonianForm decides, at the same confidence
# floor a photographed page has to clear (ADR-021, ADR-024).
#
# Unset it and the national broadcaster's feed is used. Point it at another
# https feed of Estonian news to use that instead, or set it to "off" if you
# would rather this deployment made no outbound request for a decoration. The
# other two sources — the time of year, and a draw from the graded dictionary —
# work with no network at all, so the row is never empty either way.
#
# It is NOT a recipient on /privacy, and deliberately: the request carries no
# query, no account and nothing anybody typed. It asks for a front page and
# would ask for the same one if nobody were signed in. Naming a service that
